<h1>TSafeHtml</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TSafeHtml" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TSafeHtml</tt> strips potentially dangerous HTML from its body content. It wraps the
<a href="https://htmlpurifier.org/">HTML Purifier</a> project.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
To use <tt>TSafeHtml</tt>, simply enclose the content to be secured within the <tt>TSafeHtml</tt> component tag in a template. The content may consist of both static text and PRADO controls. If the latter, the rendering result of the controls will be secured.
</p>

<h2>How it works</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The control renders its body content into a buffer rather than straight to the page. It
passes the result through HTML Purifier, and writes what comes back. Child controls are
therefore purified along with the static text, because purification happens after they
render.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TSafeHtml&gt;
  &lt;com:TLiteral Text=&lt;%= $this-&gt;Page-&gt;Comment %&gt; /&gt;
&lt;/com:TSafeHtml&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Purifier configuration</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.0.0" />

<p class="block-content">
The <tt>Config</tt> property holds an <tt>HTMLPurifier_Config</tt> object that decides
what survives purification. Leaving it unset gives the default configuration, which
caches its serialized definitions under the application's <tt>runtime/htmlpurifier</tt>
directory. That directory must be writable by the web server. The
<a href="https://htmlpurifier.org/docs">HTML Purifier documentation</a> lists the
available settings.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
<a href="?page=Controls.TextBox">TTextBox</a> carries the same property and applies it to
its <tt>SafeText</tt> value, so an application purifies input on the way in or output on
the way out.
</p>

